That's a really informative result — a bias that grows monotonically
and directionally with surface temperature (not just noisier at the
extremes, but consistently biased in one direction) points at
something different from pure random sparsity: this looks like
regression-to-the-mean / shrinkage bias, a known behavior of
MSE-trained regressors when the training distribution is imbalanced
across a variable. When examples are sparse at one end of a range, the
network — trained to minimize average squared error — has an incentive
to pull its predictions at those sparse points toward the bulk of the
distribution rather than fully committing to the correct extreme
value, because doing so reduces average loss over the (much larger)
dense middle even at some cost to the rare tails. That produces
exactly this signature: small bias near the population mean, growing
steadily as you move away from it.

Your own aside — "230 K should have ice, but whatever" — is the
tell. That means your ocean-only subset likely contains synthetic
combinations (very cold SST paired with open-water emissivity) that
are physically rare or nonexistent in reality, so there are
correspondingly few training examples there. Same likely story at 320
K — real open ocean essentially never gets that warm, so that's also a
thin tail. Restricting to ocean-only may have actively worsened this
too — cutting your training set down to a subset removes examples that
might otherwise have helped interpolate across that range, so you
traded away sample density in exchange for isolating the emissivity
variable, and the SST-tail sparsity that was always somewhat present
became more exposed.

