Where do I apply the weighting?

Three changes to your loop: precompute per-sample weights once before
training starts, slice out the batch's weights alongside Xb/Yb, pass
them into modelGradients, and use them inside the loss instead of a
plain mean. Here's exactly where each piece goes:

Before the training loop (once, using trainIdx/stempTrain from before):

    nBins = 20;
    edges = linspace(min(stempTrain), max(stempTrain), nBins+1);
    [counts, ~, binIdx] = histcounts(stempTrain, edges);
    sampleWeightTrain = 1 ./ counts(binIdx)';
    sampleWeightTrain = sampleWeightTrain / mean(sampleWeightTrain);   % (nTrainSamples x 1)

Inside the batch loop — add one line right after Yb:

    Xb = dlarray(single(Xtrain(batchIdx,:))', 'CB');
    Yb = dlarray(single(Ytrain(batchIdx,:))', 'CB');
    Wb = dlarray(single(sampleWeightTrain(batchIdx))', 'CB');   % (1 x batch) -- NEW
    
    [loss, grads] = dlfeval(@modelGradients, params, Xb, Yb, Wb, iHidden);   % Wb added

Finally modify function modelGradients.m
    function [loss, grads] = modelGradients(params, X, Y, W, iHidden)
        Ypred = forwardMLP(params, X, iHidden);
        err2 = (Ypred - Y).^2;              % (nPCA_BT x batch)
        err2 = err2 .* W;                    % W broadcasts (1 x batch) across all nPCA_BT rows
        loss = mean(err2, 'all');
        grads = dlgradient(loss, params);
    end

That's the whole change. Validation loss stays exactly as you have it
(plain mean, unweighted) — you want val loss to reflect true,
unweighted average error so it's still a fair report of real-world
performance; only the training objective should be reweighted, not the
metric you judge it by.

One check worth doing once this runs: watch train loss more carefully
now — it may look "worse" than before at first, since you're now
explicitly penalizing the rare-SST samples harder, which naturally
pulls average training loss up even while the extremes get more
accurate. That's expected and not a red flag on its own; the thing to
actually look at is whether the bias-vs-SST plot flattens, same as
before, not whether the raw MSE number went up or down.

