Good instinct to be suspicious — a genuinely flat, un-localized ~0.5-1
K floor that doesn't respond to capacity, reweighting, or emissivity
fixes is consistent with something that isn't a "the network needs
more information" problem at all, but rather a noise floor baked into
the training pairs themselves. A few concrete candidates beyond wind
speed, roughly in order of how likely I'd bet on them given what
you've described:

1. Input/label mismatch — check whether the emissivity (and other)
value fed to the network is exactly the value the RT code actually
used. You just found one bug (missing +0.3 offset). Worth asking:
could there be a different kind of bug — not a wrong value, but a
recording mismatch — where the RT solver used one value internally but
a slightly different value got logged into your training arrays? This
is extremely easy to introduce (e.g., recording emissivity before
vs. after some per-channel adjustment, or logging skin temperature
while the RT run actually used a slightly different surface
temperature for the Planck term). If the X your network sees isn't
exactly what produced the Y, that's not a learnable relationship —
it's irreducible noise, and it would show up exactly as you're
describing: scattered everywhere, no single covariate to blame, immune
to capacity/reweighting. Test: pick 20 profiles, manually trace one
value (say surf skin temp) from what's stored in your training array
all the way through to what's actually passed into the RT call, and
confirm bit-for-bit it's the same number.

2. RT solver numerical precision — check if your "truth" labels
themselves are noisy. If the RT code uses any iterative/quadrature
scheme (stream count, layer count, spectral convolution/ILS sampling),
running the same profile twice with tightened convergence settings can
reveal that the labels carry their own irreducible numerical
noise. Test: take ~20 profiles, rerun through RT with meaningfully
tighter tolerances (more streams/layers/finer spectral sampling), and
compare to the original labels. If radiances shift by an amount
comparable to your ~0.5-1 K RMSE, that's your floor — not a modeling
problem at all, a data-precision one, and no amount of ML fixes it.

3. Sigma-grid interpolation information loss on the input side. You
checked PCA truncation loss on the output spectrum (0.086 K, clean) —
but you haven't checked the equivalent for the input profiles going
through sigma-grid interpolation. If a profile has a sharp
boundary-layer inversion or humidity jump that happens to fall between
two of your fixed sigma grid points, interpolation smooths it out — a
real, silent loss of input information, and it would hit different
profiles unpredictably depending on where their sharp features land
relative to the fixed grid. That's exactly the kind of "scattered
everywhere, no clean covariate" pattern. Test: for a sample of native
profiles, interpolate to your 100-point sigma grid and then back onto
the native levels, and compute the reconstruction error against the
original — same idea as the PCA truncation check, applied to the
vertical interpolation step instead.

Given you have the RT code in hand, #2 is the cheapest and most
decisive to test right now — it directly tells you whether you're
chasing a learnable signal or a solver noise floor, in maybe 20
reruns. Want to start there?

