Here's the core difference: operational fast models like SARTA don't
regress radiance against raw (or EOF-compressed) T/Q/O3 profiles. They
regress against physically-derived predictors constructed from the
radiative transfer equation itself — things like path-integrated
absorber amounts weighted by the local temperature dependence of the
absorption coefficient, secant-weighted optical depth proxies,
layer-to-space transmittance approximations, and
reference-profile-relative perturbation terms. Instead of asking a
regressor to discover the RT physics from raw state variables (which
is what your EOF-compressed T/Q/O3 → radiance mapping is doing), they
hand it predictors that are already most of the way toward linear in
the actual quantity that matters (optical depth), because someone did
the analytic work of writing out the first-order temperature/absorber
dependence of the transmittance equation and using that as the
regression input. This is why they can get away with per-channel
linear or low-order polynomial regression and still hit 0.1-0.2 K —
the nonlinearity your DNN is working hard to approximate numerically
has already been substantially removed by the choice of predictor, not
learned from data.

That reframes your whole problem: the ~0.5 K plateau you're stuck at,
even in clean data, is very plausibly the cost of asking a generic
network to learn RT physics from scratch off raw atmospheric state,
rather than being handed physics-informed features. This is a real,
known gap between "black-box ML emulator" and "physically-motivated
fast regression," and it's exactly the kind of gap that data volume,
network depth, and reweighting can't close, because it's not a data or
capacity problem — it's an information-representation problem.

Concretely, what to try: engineer a handful of RT-motivated predictors
alongside (or instead of) your EOF coefficients, e.g.:

  Secant-weighted (view angle) integrated water vapor and ozone amount
  above each level, rather than the raw per-level values
  
  A temperature-weighted absorber amount (since absorption
  coefficients themselves depend on T — this couples T and gas amount
  rather than treating them as independent EOF channels)
  
  Perturbation from a fixed reference/mean profile rather than
  absolute values, which is closer to how these operational
  regressions are actually posed (predict the deviation in radiance
  from a reference-profile radiance, not the absolute radiance)

